  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Given the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{e}^{x}},$ which of the following statements are true?",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{2x{e}^{x}-({x}^{2}+1){e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x+1}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{e}^{x}}\\,\u27f9\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that ${e}^{x}>0$ for all values of $x.$\n\nSo, $x=1$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(0) & =-\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{{e}^{0}}=-1<0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(2) & =-\\frac{(2-1)^{2}}{{e}^{2}}=-{e}^{-2}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{\u2032}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $\u2198$ |  | $\u2198$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=1.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=1$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nTherefore, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema.",
